{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.GenWronskian","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.GenWronskian","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.ToLinearEquiv","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Nondegenerate","lean:module:Mathlib.RingTheory.Polynomial.Wronskian","lean:module:Mathlib.RingTheory.EuclideanDomain","lean:module:Mathlib.RingTheory.Polynomial.Content"],"declarations":18,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.genWronskian_eq_zero_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.genWronskian_eq_zero_iff","kind":"theorem","line":390,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/GenWronskian.lean#L390-L400","doc":"**Linear dependence over the constants is exactly a vanishing generalized Wronskian**,\nin characteristic zero.\n\nThis is the form Roth's lemma consumes: a *non-zero* generalized Wronskian is a certificate\nof linear independence. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.exists_dependence_of_genWronskian_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.exists_dependence_of_genWronskian_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":321,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/GenWronskian.lean#L321-L388","doc":"**The converse: a vanishing generalized Wronskian forces a constant linear dependence.**\n\nInduction on `m`.  Either the leading minor already degenerates — and the inductive\nhypothesis finishes, extending by zero — or it does not, and then the kernel vector's last\nentry is non-zero, `sum_wronskian_combo_eq_zero` produces a kernel vector of the\nnon-degenerate minor, which is therefore zero, which is exactly the hypothesis of\n`exists_constant_dependence_of_wronskians_vanish`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.exists_constant_dependence_of_wronskians_vanish","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.exists_constant_dependence_of_wronskians_vanish","kind":"theorem","line":286,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/GenWronskian.lean#L286-L319","doc":"**Steps 4–5.**  Once every `wronskian (c l) (c j)` vanishes and `c l ≠ 0`, the polynomial\nkernel vector collapses to a **constant** one — and that constant vector is non-zero.\n\n⚠ **The non-triviality is the part that is easy to lose.**  Cancelling `c l` gives\n`∑ⱼ C λⱼ · fⱼ = 0` whatever `λ` is, and `λ = 0` satisfies that vacuously — so a proof that\nstops at cancellation has proved nothing.  What rules it out is the relation at `j = l`:\n`c l = C (λ l) * c l` with `c l ≠ 0` in a domain forces `λ l = 1`.  Hence `λ ≠ 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.exists_kernel_of_genWronskian_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.exists_kernel_of_genWronskian_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":276,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/GenWronskian.lean#L276-L284","doc":"**Step 1.**  A vanishing generalized Wronskian yields a non-zero *polynomial* kernel\nvector — the same `Matrix.exists_mulVec_eq_zero_iff` the forward direction uses, read in the\nother direction. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.sum_succ_split","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.sum_succ_split","kind":"theorem","line":268,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/GenWronskian.lean#L268-L274","doc":"Peeling the last index off a Wronskian-style sum.  `Fin.init` is definitionally\nprecomposition with `castSucc`, which is why this closes by `rfl` after the split. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.mulVec_wronskianMatrix_eq_zero_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.mulVec_wronskianMatrix_eq_zero_iff","kind":"theorem","line":255,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/GenWronskian.lean#L255-L266","doc":"The kernel condition on the Wronskian matrix, spelled out as derivative equations.\nThe transposition of the two factors is the only content: `mulVec` multiplies on the right,\nthe derivative systems above are written with the coefficient on the left. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.sum_wronskian_combo_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.sum_wronskian_combo_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":227,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/GenWronskian.lean#L227-L249","doc":"**The Wronskian combination of a kernel vector is again a kernel vector** (one row\nshorter), and its `l`-th entry is zero.\n\n`∑ⱼ W(c_l, cⱼ) · fⱼ⁽ⁱ⁾ = c_l · (∑ⱼ cⱼ′ fⱼ⁽ⁱ⁾) − c_l′ · (∑ⱼ cⱼ fⱼ⁽ⁱ⁾)`, and both brackets\nvanish — the first by derivative descent, the second by hypothesis.\n\n⚠ **This is what removes the need for the function field.**  The classical proof normalises\nthe kernel vector so that its last entry is `1`, which requires dividing and so forces the\nargument into `RatFunc K`.  Multiplying by `c_l` instead of dividing achieves the same\ncancellation inside `R[X]`, and the resulting entries are literally `wronskian (c l) (c j)`\n— so `exists_dependence_of_wronskian_eq_zero` above applies to them directly. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.sum_derivative_descent_witness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.sum_derivative_descent_witness","kind":"theorem","line":214,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/GenWronskian.lean#L214-L225","doc":"⚠ **The hypothesis is satisfiable with a non-constant `c`**, so the lemma is not about\nan empty range.\n\nAt `k = 0` the conclusion quantifies over `i < 0` and says nothing, so the content lives\nentirely at `k ≥ 1` — and a reader could reasonably wonder whether the hypothesis can hold\nthat far with `c` non-constant, since for *independent* `f` it cannot. It can when the `f`\nare dependent, which is exactly the situation the induction is in: `f = ![X, X]` with\n`c = ![X, −X]` annihilates every derivative row. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.sum_derivative_of_forall_sum_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.sum_derivative_of_forall_sum_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":190,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/GenWronskian.lean#L190-L212","doc":"**Derivative descent.**  If a coefficient vector annihilates the first `k + 1`\nderivative rows, its own derivative annihilates the first `k`.\n\nDifferentiating the `i`-th relation gives `∑ⱼ cⱼ′ fⱼ⁽ⁱ⁾ + ∑ⱼ cⱼ fⱼ⁽ⁱ⁺¹⁾ = 0`, and the\nsecond sum *is* the `(i+1)`-st relation, hence zero — **which is exactly why the hypothesis\nmust reach one row further than the conclusion.**\n\nIterating this is what forces every `cⱼ` to be constant in the classical proof. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.exists_dependence_of_wronskian_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.exists_dependence_of_wronskian_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":136,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/GenWronskian.lean#L136-L175","doc":"**The converse at `m = 2` in characteristic zero:** a vanishing Wronskian forces a\nlinear dependence with constant coefficients.\n\nDivide by the gcd to reach coprime `a₁, b₁`; the scaling identity kills `g²`, so\n`W(a₁, b₁) = 0`; coprimality turns that into `a₁' = b₁' = 0`; characteristic zero makes both\nconstant; and `b₁ · a − a₁ · b = 0` is then the dependence.\n\n⚠ Stated for a **field** because the gcd step needs one. The general-`m` statement, which\nRoth's lemma actually consumes, is an induction with this as its base; it is proved below as\n`exists_dependence_of_genWronskian_eq_zero`, and consumes this lemma directly. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.wronskian_mul_mul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.wronskian_mul_mul","kind":"theorem","line":128,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/GenWronskian.lean#L128-L134","doc":"**The Wronskian scales quadratically under a common factor**: `W(ga, gb) = g² W(a, b)`.\n\nThis is what reduces the general case to the coprime one — divide out the gcd. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.genWronskian_one_X","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.genWronskian_one_X","kind":"theorem","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/GenWronskian.lean#L109-L118","doc":"⚠ **Non-vacuity: a non-zero generalized Wronskian occurs.**\n\n`genWronskian_eq_zero_of_dependent` is used through its contrapositive — *non-zero\nWronskian ⟹ linearly independent* — which certifies nothing if the Wronskian is always\nzero. It is not: `1` and `X` give the identity matrix and Wronskian `1`.\n\nWorth pinning because the vanishing theorem alone is consistent with a definition that\nreturns `0` identically, and every downstream use would still typecheck. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.genWronskian_eq_zero_of_dependent","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.genWronskian_eq_zero_of_dependent","kind":"theorem","line":77,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/GenWronskian.lean#L77-L107","doc":"**A linear dependence kills the generalized Wronskian.**\n\nIf `∑ⱼ cⱼ · fⱼ = 0` with the constants `c` not all zero, then differentiating `i` times\ngives `∑ⱼ cⱼ · fⱼ⁽ⁱ⁾ = 0` for every `i` — that is exactly the statement that the constant\nvector `c` is in the kernel of the Wronskian matrix, so the determinant vanishes.\n\nThe contrapositive is what Roth's lemma consumes: a non-zero generalized Wronskian\ncertifies linear independence. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.genWronskian_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.genWronskian_one","kind":"theorem","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/GenWronskian.lean#L72-L75","doc":"A one-polynomial Wronskian is the polynomial itself. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.genWronskian_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.genWronskian_two","kind":"theorem","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/GenWronskian.lean#L64-L70","doc":"⚠ **Agreement with Mathlib's two-polynomial Wronskian.**\n\nWithout this the generalisation would only *resemble* the existing definition. With it, the\n`m = 2` case is pinned: `genWronskian ![a, b] = wronskian a b` on the nose. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.genWronskian","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.genWronskian","kind":"def","line":60,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/GenWronskian.lean#L60-L62","doc":"The **generalized Wronskian** of `m` polynomials. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.wronskianMatrix_apply","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.wronskianMatrix_apply","kind":"theorem","line":56,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/GenWronskian.lean#L56-L58","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:Polynomial.wronskianMatrix","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"Polynomial.wronskianMatrix","kind":"def","line":50,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/GenWronskian.lean#L50-L54","doc":"The **Wronskian matrix** of `m` polynomials: entry `(i, j)` is the `i`-th derivative of\n`f j`, so row `i` is the `i`-th derivative of the whole family. ","signatureChunk":0}]}